Hi,

JRIEngine.parse fails to report a parse problem. I think this is due to 
the test:

if( pr == 0 )

that should be one of these instead:

if( pr == R_NilValue )
if( pr == 0 || pr == R_NilValue )

Even better would be to actually get the parse error, but I think we 
already mentioned this before and this needs some changes in R too.

Could the exception created be of a subclass of REngineException (say 
ParseErrorException) so that it is easier to disambiguate from other 
exceptions but still is catchable by catch( REngineException e) ... 
something like

package org.rosuda.REngine.exceptions ;
import org.rosuda.REngine.REngineException ;

/**
  * Parse error
  */
public class ParseErrorException extends REngineException {

	private String errorMessage = "" ;

	public ParseException( REngine e, String errorMessage ){
		super( e, "parse error : " + errorMessage ) ;
		this.error = error ;
	}

	public String getErrorMessage(){
		return errorMessage ;
	}

}

so that later when there is a way to capture the parse error from R, we 
get a more useful error message.

